<main class="wrap">
<section class="sec">
  <h2>Who Is Nezha?</h2>
  <p>Nezha is a young deity-like character from Chinese literature and folklore. His best-known classic source is the novel <b>*Investiture of the Gods* (封神演义)</b>, and he also appears in *Journey to the West*. He's often depicted as a powerful child or youth.</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>What He Represents</h2>
  <p>Nezha is commonly read as a symbol of <b>youthful rebellion</b> and <b>defiance of fate</b> — standing up against authority, and sometimes against his own destiny. *(This is one common interpretation; emphasis shifts between versions.)*</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>The Lotus Rebirth</h2>
  <p>One famous part of his legend is a <b>rebirth with a body made from lotus</b>, an image of transformation and renewal. *(How and why this happens differs across versions, so treat it as legend rather than fixed history.)*</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>There Isn't One "True" Version</h2>
  <p>Because Nezha appears across several classic works and many modern films and shows, details vary a lot. It's best to say "in one version…" rather than presenting any single telling — including a recent movie — as the definitive story.</p>
</section>

